<title>USA: Summer rains welcome in Florida citrus belt - FASS.</title>
<headline>Summer rains welcome in Florida citrus belt - FASS.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-06-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Much-needed summer rains have arrived in Florida's citrus belt, Florida Agricultural Statistics Service (FASS) said in it's weekly Weather and Crop News report.</p>
<p>FASS reported that ponds, lakes and streams in the region are low, but are quickly being refilled by the daily showers and thunderstorms.</p>
<p>New-crop fruit continues to make good progress, according to FASS. Current crop Valencia oranges remaining to be picked are in good condition.</p>
<p>Caretakers continue to cut cover crops, spray, fertilize and apply summer herbicides. Pushing and burning dead trees is active in all areas.</p>
<p>FASS reported an estimated 3,860,000 boxes of Valencia oranges were harvested in the week ended June 9, down from 5,776,000 in the week ended June 2.</p>
<p>-- New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640</p>
</text>
